{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>בעצלתים</b>. It is known that every word that is dual is plural with it — עינים, רגלים, ידים, שוקים, שינים — because they are in two sets, like רחים, and likewise שמים, and one who understands the secret of the spheres will testify likewise, and likewise פעמים, also מים, and it has a hidden and sealed secret, and likewise all tablets that have decks like two tablets, מעקש דרכים, and the end of the verse יפול באחת indicates this, and so R. Moshe ha-Kohen, of blessed memory, explained: צהרים. If so, the word עצלתים would refer back to ידים, as though he said 'in idle hands,' and it is an elliptical expression, and המקרה in this place is a noun. And some say it is a verb, like המקרה in מים עליותיו המה קרוהו, and all are from the heavy [pi'el] construct, dageshed, and the resh ought to receive a dagesh, and he would say that המקרה, through the idleness of his hands, did a lowly deed — and this is far-fetched, because ימך is from the nif'al construct, like ידל כבוד יעקב, and it is of the doubled-root [hitpa'el] verbs." "<b>ובשפלות ידים</b>. Its meaning is poverty and want. And ידלוף is from the root of דלף, 'dripping,' and its meaning is the drops that come down. And this verse is joined to the verse above, as though he says about that king and the officers who busy themselves with eating and are idle to attend to the affairs of the kingship — behold, two things will befall them because of their idleness: one, that their kingdom will be ruined, like a house that collapses little by little, and the beams will sag; and the second thing, that they do not take care to add wealth and store it in time of need — and this is the meaning of ובשפלות ידים."], :book :ecclesiastes, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {0 "The text 'ימך מבנין נפעל' is difficult: ימך ('sinks, declines') is being labeled nif'al, and then called מפעלי הכפל, which normally denotes hitpa'el; the apparent contradiction is translated as found. Also, 'הבנין הכבד הדגוש' refers to pi'el by the standard terminology of the Hebrew grammarians; the gloss is inserted but the original ambiguity is preserved.", 1 "The word 'המקרה' is rendered 'the beams' following Ibn Ezra's own derivation in comment 0 linking it to sagging roof-structure; he appears to read it here as a noun referring to the building's framework."}, :source-witnesses [["Wikisource" "https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%90%D7%91%D7%9F_%D7%A2%D7%96%D7%A8%D7%90_%D7%A2%D7%9C_%D7%A7%D7%94%D7%9C%D7%AA"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:29:13.288460745Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}